co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15822.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>108th of the 108 Parshwanaths - Lord Shri Antariksha Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: The image of Lord Shri Antariksha Parshwanath is black in color, seated in the half-lotus (ardha-padmasana) posture, 36 inches high (42 inches including the serpent hoods) and 30 inches wide. This image is made of sand and hovers in mid-air — there is a gap between the image and its pedestal through which a thin muslin cloth can be passed from one end to the other beneath the image. This image is 11,80,000 years old, dating from the era of the 20th Tirthankara, Lord Munisuvrat Swami. It is one of the ancient images of Lord Parshwanath and is located in the basement of the temple.</p><p>Location: This temple is on the outskirts of the village of Shirpur in Maharashtra.</p><p>History: To this day, this ancient image remains suspended in mid-air. Devotees pray to this image through the &quot;Sakal Tirth Vandana Stotra,&quot; recited every day during the morning pratikraman.</p><p>The Story of the Origin of This Image: King Khardushan, brother-in-law of King Ravana, was sent on an important mission to another place. At midday, King Khardushan stopped to rest in the village of Vingoli. He had taken a vow to eat only after worshipping an image of a Tirthankara, so he fashioned an image of a Tirthankara out of sand and consecrated it while chanting the Navkar Mahamantra. After worship, he immersed the image in a nearby well. A deity residing in the well took the image in his hands, transformed it into a stone image, and worshipped it for a long time thereafter.</p><p>King Shripal, of the lunar dynasty of the city of Alchapur, suffered from leprosy, and all remedies to cure the disease had failed. One day the king went outside the city, and, feeling thirsty, came upon a well; he washed his hands, feet, and face with its water, drank from it, and fell asleep. Upon waking the next morning, he found that the leprosy on his hands, feet, and face had been cured. Learning of the miraculous power of the well water, the king returned to the well and bathed in it, and was completely cured of his disease. When the king prayed in gratitude, a deity appeared and told him that this miracle was due to an image of Lord Parshwanath that lay within the well. The king asked the deity for the image; moved by the king&#x27;s devotion, Dharanendra Dev granted it to him and explained how to bring it out of the well. The next day, following the deity&#x27;s instructions, the image was retrieved from the well.</p><p>The king placed the image on a chariot drawn by two newborn (seven-day-old) calves. After traveling some distance, the king turned to look back to see whether the chariot was following; the moment he looked back, the chariot stopped instantly. The image was left suspended in mid-air, and the chariot returned empty. Great efforts were made to move the image further, but it would not budge, and it came to rest suspended in the air beneath a tree. It is said that the image hovered seven hasta above the ground — so much so that a man on horseback, or a woman carrying a water pot on her head, could pass beneath it. This is why the image came to be known as &quot;Antariksha Parshwanath&quot; (Parshwanath of the sky/mid-air). Due to the influence of the Kali Yuga, only a thin cloth can now be passed beneath the image; even today, however, the image remains suspended in mid-air and does not touch the ground. The Jain sangh built a beautiful temple here.</p><p>The image of Lord Shri Antariksha Parshwanath was consecrated in this temple on the fifth day of the bright half of Magh in Vikram Samvat 1142, by the auspicious hands of Maladhari Acharya Shri Abhaydev Surishwarji. King Shripal founded a city here named Shripur, known today as Shirpur. This pilgrimage site became very famous, and the temple has been rebuilt many times over the centuries. Miracles continue to occur here. Shri Bhavvijay Gani had lost his eyesight and was deeply distressed; Acharya Shri Vijaydevsuriji advised him to pray to the goddess Padmavati. Pleased by his prayers, the goddess appeared and told him to worship Lord Shri Antariksha Parshwanath. The Ganivarya came with the Jain sangh to Antariksha Tirth, and upon reciting the stotra there, his eyesight was restored. Under the Ganivarya Bhavvijayji&#x27;s direction, the image was reconsecrated on the fifth day of the bright half of Chaitra in Vikram Samvat 1715. A fair is held here every year on the third day of the bright half of Falgun and on the tenth day of the dark half of Paush.</p><p>Other Temples: Near this temple is a beautiful, artistic temple of Lord Vighnahar Parshwanath.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: This image is very ancient, artistic, and magnificent. The image of Lord Shri Antariksha Parshwanath is located in the basement, and it is beautiful and striking.</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station to this pilgrimage site is Washim, 19 kilometers away, and it is 72 kilometers from Akola. The village of Shirpur is 11 km from the Malegaon-Hingoli-Balapur road. Bus service and private vehicles are available. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here, and there is also a Digambar dharmashala nearby.</p><p>Scripture References: Lord Antariksha Parshwanath is mentioned in the &quot;Vividh Tirth Kalp,&quot; the &quot;Prabandh Panchashati,&quot; the &quot;Shri Antariksha Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; the &quot;365 Shri Parshwanath Jin Nammala,&quot; the &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; the &quot;Shri Bhatev Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;108 Namgarbhit Shri Godiji Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Saubhagya Mahavakya,&quot; the &quot;108 Namgarbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Antariksha Parshwanath Stotra,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Nammala,&quot; the &quot;Tirthamala,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Prabhu ke 108 Nam Chhand,&quot; and others. Antariksha Parshwanath is also mentioned in the 14th-century Marathi text &quot;Smriti Sthali.&quot; The main temple of Shri Antariksha Parshwanath is in Shirpur, Maharashtra. An image of Antariksha Parshwanath is also found at Jiravala Tirth, Bhildiyaji Tirth, the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai, Sherisa Tirth, Kesariyajinagar in Palitana, the Shri Sambhavnath temple in Karad, and at many other places across India.</p><p>Praise (Stuti): O Lord, who filled Khar and Dushan with wonder at the very sight of you, crown of Shirpur, dwelling suspended in mid-air; you grant sight to the blind, and open the door to liberation. &quot;With devotion I bow to Shri Antariksha Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Antariksha Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Antariksha Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Shri Antariksha Parshwanath Maharaj Sansthan, Post: Shirpur, Taluka: Malegaon, District: Washim - 444504, State: Maharashtra, India. Phone: 0754-234005, Mobile: 9158871999.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-134\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Girua Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-136\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Dukhbhanjan Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\"><p>१०८ पार्श्वनाथ त